const prompts=[
{unit:1,title:"Site, Situation, and Scale",scenario:"A city developed where two rivers meet. Later, it grew because a major interstate highway and rail line connected it to nearby markets. A regional map shows the city as a transportation hub, while a local map shows different neighborhoods with unequal access to services.",
 parts:[
 ["A","Identify","Identify the geographic concept that describes the physical characteristics of the city's location.","Site."],
 ["B","Define","Define situation.","Situation is the location of a place relative to other places and connections, such as trade routes, highways, rivers, or nearby markets."],
 ["C","Describe","Describe one way the city's site may have supported its early growth.","The rivers provided water access, fertile land, or transportation routes that helped people settle and trade there."],
 ["D","Explain","Explain how the city's situation helped it grow economically.","The interstate and rail connections helped the city grow because they improved transportation, trade, and access to markets, attracting businesses and workers."],
 ["E","Explain","Explain how changing the scale of analysis from regional to local could change what a geographer sees.","Changing from regional to local scale changes the interpretation because the regional map shows broad transportation connections, while the local map reveals neighborhood-level differences in services or access."],
 ["F","Apply","Apply GIS to this scenario by explaining one layer a geographer could add to study inequality in the city.","A geographer could add a transit, income, grocery-store, or school-access layer in GIS to compare which neighborhoods have fewer services."],
 ["G","Evaluate","Evaluate one planning decision that could improve access for residents in underserved neighborhoods.","Expanding bus routes or adding services in underserved neighborhoods could improve access because residents would have better connections to jobs, food, schools, or health care."]]},
{unit:2,title:"Population and Migration",scenario:"A country has a rising median age, low fertility, and a shrinking workforce. At the same time, some leaders support immigration to fill labor shortages, while others worry about pressure on housing and services.",
 parts:[
 ["A","Identify","Identify one demographic indicator that shows an aging population.","A high elderly dependency ratio, a top-heavy population pyramid, low fertility rate, or rising median age."],
 ["B","Define","Define dependency ratio.","Dependency ratio compares the number of dependents, such as children and elderly people, to the working-age population."],
 ["C","Describe","Describe one economic challenge caused by an aging population.","An aging population can increase pension costs, health-care spending, or labor shortages."],
 ["D","Explain","Explain how immigration could help address a shrinking workforce.","Immigration could help because working-age migrants fill labor shortages and contribute taxes, reducing pressure on the existing workforce."],
 ["E","Explain","Explain one possible social or political challenge connected to increased immigration.","Increased immigration may create political tension because some residents may worry about cultural change, competition for housing, or pressure on public services."],
 ["F","Apply","Apply the Demographic Transition Model by identifying the stage most likely associated with low birth rates and low death rates.","Stage 4, or sometimes Stage 5, is associated with low birth rates and low death rates."],
 ["G","Evaluate","Evaluate one policy a government could use to respond to an aging population.","A government could provide childcare support, encourage immigration, raise the retirement age, or invest in automation because these policies can increase the workforce or reduce dependency pressure."]]},
{unit:3,title:"Cultural Diffusion",scenario:"A cultural trait begins in one hearth, spreads through migration, and is later adapted by businesses and communities in different countries. In some places, the trait changes to fit local languages, religions, or food preferences.",
 parts:[
 ["A","Identify","Identify the term for the place where a cultural trait begins.","A cultural hearth."],
 ["B","Define","Define relocation diffusion.","Relocation diffusion is the spread of a cultural trait when people move and bring that trait with them."],
 ["C","Describe","Describe one example of stimulus diffusion.","A global restaurant changing its menu to fit local religious or cultural food preferences is an example of stimulus diffusion."],
 ["D","Explain","Explain how migration can spread culture.","Migration spreads culture because migrants bring languages, religions, foods, and customs with them to new places."],
 ["E","Explain","Explain how globalization can speed up cultural diffusion.","Globalization speeds up diffusion because communication technology, trade, media, and travel allow cultural traits to spread faster across long distances."],
 ["F","Apply","Apply the concept of lingua franca to global cultural interaction.","A lingua franca, such as English in global business, helps different language groups communicate and exchange cultural or economic ideas."],
 ["G","Evaluate","Evaluate one effect of cultural diffusion on local cultures.","Cultural diffusion can increase diversity and access to new ideas, but it can also weaken local traditions because global cultural traits may replace or change local practices."]]},
{unit:4,title:"Devolution and Political Power",scenario:"A culturally distinct region within a country is demanding more autonomy from the central government. The region has its own language, a strong regional identity, and claims that national policies do not meet local needs.",
 parts:[
 ["A","Identify","Identify the political process described in the scenario.","Devolution."],
 ["B","Define","Define sovereignty.","Sovereignty is the authority of a state to govern itself and control its territory without outside interference."],
 ["C","Describe","Describe one cultural factor that could lead to devolution.","A distinct language, religion, ethnicity, or regional identity can lead to devolutionary pressure."],
 ["D","Explain","Explain how regional identity can create devolutionary pressure.","Regional identity can create devolutionary pressure because people may feel more loyal to their region than the central government and demand local control."],
 ["E","Explain","Explain how a federal system could reduce devolutionary pressure.","A federal system could reduce devolutionary pressure because it gives regions some political authority, allowing local needs to be addressed without full independence."],
 ["F","Apply","Apply the concept of centrifugal force to this scenario.","The distinct regional identity is a centrifugal force because it pulls people away from national unity and toward regional autonomy."],
 ["G","Evaluate","Evaluate one policy a central government could use to keep the state unified.","The government could grant regional autonomy, protect language rights, or share revenue because these policies address grievances while keeping the country together."]]},
{unit:5,title:"Agricultural Land Use",scenario:"Farmers in a region choose different types of agriculture based on their distance from a central market city. Perishable products are produced closer to the city, while less perishable crops are produced farther away.",
 parts:[
 ["A","Identify","Identify the agricultural land-use model described in the scenario.","The Von Thünen model."],
 ["B","Define","Define commercial agriculture.","Commercial agriculture is farming primarily for sale in markets rather than mainly for family consumption."],
 ["C","Describe","Describe the pattern of agricultural land use in the Von Thünen model.","Agricultural activities are arranged in rings around a central market, with perishable or expensive-to-transport goods closer to the market."],
 ["D","Explain","Explain why dairy or market gardening is located close to the market.","Dairy and market gardening are close to the market because these products spoil quickly and cost more to transport over long distances."],
 ["E","Explain","Explain one limitation of the Von Thünen model in the real world.","The model is limited because real landscapes include roads, rivers, mountains, government policies, refrigeration, and multiple markets that change land-use patterns."],
 ["F","Apply","Apply the Green Revolution to explain one change in agricultural production.","The Green Revolution increased production because high-yield seeds, fertilizers, irrigation, and machinery allowed farmers to grow more food."],
 ["G","Evaluate","Evaluate one negative consequence of modern commercial agriculture.","Modern commercial agriculture can harm the environment because monoculture, fertilizer use, and irrigation may cause soil depletion, water pollution, or water shortages."]]},
{unit:6,title:"Gentrification and Urban Change",scenario:"A low-income urban neighborhood receives new private investment, rising rents, renovated housing, and new businesses catering to wealthier residents. Some residents welcome improvements, while others fear displacement.",
 parts:[
 ["A","Identify","Identify the urban process described in the scenario.","Gentrification."],
 ["B","Define","Define gentrification.","Gentrification is a process in which investment and higher-income residents move into a lower-income neighborhood, raising property values and rents."],
 ["C","Describe","Describe one positive effect of gentrification.","Gentrification can bring new businesses, improved housing, increased tax revenue, or better infrastructure."],
 ["D","Explain","Explain one negative effect of gentrification on longtime residents.","Gentrification can displace longtime residents because rising rents and property taxes make the neighborhood unaffordable."],
 ["E","Explain","Explain how redlining or past housing discrimination can shape present-day urban inequality.","Redlining can shape present-day inequality because neighborhoods denied loans and investment often experienced lower property values, fewer services, and concentrated poverty over time."],
 ["F","Apply","Apply the concept of scale of analysis to this urban issue.","At the city scale, the neighborhood may look like an area of new investment, but at the local scale GIS data may reveal displacement, rent increases, or unequal access to services."],
 ["G","Evaluate","Evaluate one policy that could reduce displacement from gentrification.","Affordable housing requirements, rent stabilization, community land trusts, or tenant protections could reduce displacement because they help longtime residents remain in the neighborhood despite rising costs."]]},
{unit:7,title:"Globalization and Development",scenario:"A company moves manufacturing operations to a lower-income country while keeping its headquarters, research, and marketing in a high-income country. Supporters argue this creates jobs, while critics argue it increases dependency and inequality.",
 parts:[
 ["A","Identify","Identify the economic process described in the scenario.","Outsourcing or offshoring."],
 ["B","Define","Define HDI.","HDI, or Human Development Index, measures development using income, education, and life expectancy."],
 ["C","Describe","Describe one benefit of outsourcing for the lower-income country.","Outsourcing can bring manufacturing jobs, foreign investment, industrial growth, or export income."],
 ["D","Explain","Explain one negative effect of outsourcing on workers in the high-income country.","Outsourcing can hurt workers in the high-income country because manufacturing jobs may move overseas, causing unemployment or deindustrialization."],
 ["E","Explain","Explain how Wallerstein's world-systems theory would interpret this relationship.","World-systems theory would interpret this relationship as unequal because core countries keep high-value activities while peripheral or semi-peripheral countries provide lower-wage labor."],
 ["F","Apply","Apply Rostow's model to explain how industrialization might affect a lower-income country.","Using Rostow's model, industrialization could help a country move toward takeoff because manufacturing investment increases production, jobs, and infrastructure."],
 ["G","Evaluate","Evaluate one strategy a lower-income country could use to increase development.","A country could invest in education, infrastructure, higher-value industries, or fair trade policies because these strategies reduce dependency and increase local economic benefits."]]}
];

const quiz=[["Unit 1","Which term refers to the physical characteristics of a place, like landforms or water access?",["Site","Situation","Scale","Region"],"Site","Site means the physical features of a location — landforms, climate, water access. Situation means where a place is RELATIVE to other places."],["Unit 1","A city sits at a major river crossing on an interstate highway. This is an example of its...",["Situation","Site","Formal region","Density"],"Situation","Situation = relative location and connections to other places (like highways and rivers that link it to other cities)."],["Unit 1","A region defined by a shared measurable characteristic (like a climate zone) is called a...",["Formal region","Functional region","Perceptual region","Vernacular region"],"Formal region","A formal region has a shared measurable characteristic. Example: the Sahara Desert or the Corn Belt."],["Unit 1","A pizza delivery zone centered on one restaurant is best described as a...",["Functional region","Formal region","Perceptual region","Cultural hearth"],"Functional region","A functional region is organized around a node and the flows connected to it. Here, the restaurant is the node and its delivery trips define the surrounding service area."],["Unit 1","GIS is mainly used to...",["Store, analyze, and map spatial data","Predict birth rates only","Measure HDI","Create new countries"],"Store, analyze, and map spatial data","GIS (Geographic Information Systems) helps geographers layer and analyze spatial information from multiple data sources."],["Unit 2","A country in Stage 2 of the DTM usually has...",["High birth rates and falling death rates","Low birth rates and low death rates","High death rates and low birth rates","Negative natural increase"],"High birth rates and falling death rates","Stage 2 has RAPID population growth — death rates fall (better medicine/food) but birth rates stay high."],["Unit 2","A population pyramid with a very wide base usually shows...",["High birth rates","An aging population","Low fertility","High elderly dependency"],"High birth rates","A wide base = many young children = high fertility and high birth rates."],["Unit 2","Which of these is a PUSH factor for migration?",["War or conflict","Higher wages","Better schools","Political freedom"],"War or conflict","Push factors PUSH people out of a place. Pull factors ATTRACT people to a new place."],["Unit 2","Which of these is a PULL factor?",["Drought","Conflict","Job opportunities","Religious persecution"],"Job opportunities","Job opportunities attract migrants. That makes it a pull factor."],["Unit 2","A high dependency ratio means...",["Many dependents compared to working-age people","Everyone is working age","Birth rates are zero","Migration has stopped"],"Many dependents compared to working-age people","Dependency ratio compares dependents (children + elderly) to working-age people. High = more economic pressure."],["Unit 3","A cultural hearth is...",["The place where a cultural trait originates","A boundary between countries","A type of suburb","A farming model"],"The place where a cultural trait originates","A hearth is the origin or birthplace of a cultural trait (language, religion, food, etc.)."],["Unit 3","Migrants bringing their home language to a new country is an example of...",["Relocation diffusion","Stimulus diffusion","Contagious diffusion","Hierarchical diffusion"],"Relocation diffusion","Relocation diffusion = the people MOVE and physically carry their culture with them."],["Unit 3","A fashion trend spreading from NYC and LA to smaller towns is an example of...",["Hierarchical diffusion","Relocation diffusion","Devolution","Stimulus diffusion"],"Hierarchical diffusion","Hierarchical diffusion spreads from large/important places to smaller ones — following a hierarchy."],["Unit 3","McDonald's adding a McSpicy Paneer burger in India to fit local tastes is an example of...",["Stimulus diffusion","Contagious diffusion","Relocation diffusion","Distance decay"],"Stimulus diffusion","Stimulus diffusion: the IDEA spreads, but it ADAPTS or changes in the new place. The brand spreads, but the menu item changes."],["Unit 3","A lingua franca is...",["A shared language used for communication between different groups","A local dialect only spoken in one region","A type of ethnic religion","A physical geographic boundary"],"A shared language used for communication between different groups","A lingua franca allows people who speak different native languages to communicate. Example: English is used as a lingua franca in global business."],["Unit 4","For a territory to be a STATE, it must have...",["Defined territory, population, government, and sovereignty","Only one language","No neighboring countries","Only one ethnic group"],"Defined territory, population, government, and sovereignty","A state (country) needs: territory with borders, a population, a government, and sovereignty (supreme authority)."],["Unit 4","A NATION is...",["A group of people with shared cultural identity","The same thing as a state","A central business district","A farming region"],"A group of people with shared cultural identity","Nation = people with shared culture, history, language, or identity. State = political unit. They are NOT the same."],["Unit 4","Devolution is best described as...",["Transfer of power from central to regional governments","Power moving only to global organizations","Drawing unfair voting districts","Ending all international trade"],"Transfer of power from central to regional governments","Devolution shifts power DOWNWARD from the central government to regional or local levels."],["Unit 4","Gerrymandering is used to influence...",["Political representation in elections","Agricultural output","Population growth","Cultural diffusion"],"Political representation in elections","Gerrymandering manipulates voting district boundaries to give one party an advantage."],["Unit 4","A centripetal force...",["Unifies and strengthens a state","Pulls a state apart","Always causes war","Reduces sovereignty"],"Unifies and strengthens a state","Centripetal = CENTER-seeking = brings people together. Centrifugal = pulls APART. Remember: CENTRIpetal = CENTER."],["Unit 5","Von Thünen's model explains...",["Agricultural land use patterns around a market","Political voting districts","Population aging","Religious diffusion"],"Agricultural land use patterns around a market","The model predicts WHAT gets farmed WHERE based on distance from the market and transportation costs."],["Unit 5","In Von Thünen's model, dairy is close to the market because...",["It is perishable and expensive to transport","It is always grown in deserts","It is not profitable","It only grows near cities"],"It is perishable and expensive to transport","Dairy spoils quickly AND is heavy. Both factors make transporting it far too costly, so farms locate near the market."],["Unit 5","Subsistence agriculture means...",["Growing food mainly for your own family or local use","Growing food only for global export","Factory-scale food production","Urban farming only"],"Growing food mainly for your own family or local use","Subsistence = survival farming. You grow what your family eats, not to sell on a large scale."],["Unit 5","The Green Revolution increased food production mainly through...",["High-yield seeds, fertilizers, irrigation, and technology","Reducing technology","Ending irrigation","Only traditional farming"],"High-yield seeds, fertilizers, irrigation, and technology","The Green Revolution (1960s–70s) used science and technology to dramatically increase crop yields in developing countries."],["Unit 5","A food desert is an area with...",["Limited access to affordable, healthy food","No political power","No highway access","No religious buildings"],"Limited access to affordable, healthy food","Food deserts exist in many low-income urban and rural areas where grocery stores are scarce."],["Unit 6","Gentrification usually involves...",["New investment, rising rents, and possible displacement of longtime residents","Only rural farming changes","Lower land values","Less business activity"],"New investment, rising rents, and possible displacement of longtime residents","Gentrification = private investment flows into a lower-income neighborhood → rents rise → longtime residents may be displaced."],["Unit 6","The CBD is...",["The central business district — the commercial core of a city","The countryside boundary district","A rural farming zone","A type of diffusion"],"The central business district — the commercial core of a city","The CBD has the highest land values, tallest buildings, and most business activity in a city."],["Unit 6","Urban sprawl is...",["Low-density expansion of a city outward into surrounding land","A decline in birth rates","A type of cultural diffusion","An agricultural practice"],"Low-density expansion of a city outward into surrounding land","Sprawl = the city spreads outward with low-density development (strip malls, subdivisions) that requires cars."],["Unit 6","Smart growth policies are designed to...",["Reduce sprawl and encourage sustainable, walkable development","Encourage unlimited outward expansion","Remove public transportation","End all zoning rules"],"Reduce sprawl and encourage sustainable, walkable development","Smart growth promotes compact, transit-friendly, environmentally sustainable urban development."],["Unit 6","An edge city is...",["A large suburban area with offices, retail, and entertainment","The original downtown area only","A small rural village","A refugee settlement"],"A large suburban area with offices, retail, and entertainment","Edge cities develop OUTSIDE the traditional CBD, usually near highway interchanges, with their own job centers."],["Unit 7","HDI measures development using...",["Income, education, and life expectancy","Only GDP","Only population density","Only urbanization rate"],"Income, education, and life expectancy","HDI is better than GDP alone because it captures three dimensions of human development, not just economic output."],["Unit 7","Rostow's model describes...",["Stages that all countries can move through to develop economically","Agricultural zones around a market","How religions spread","How voting districts are drawn"],"Stages that all countries can move through to develop economically","Rostow argued every country can develop by moving through five stages (like traditional society → mass consumption)."],["Unit 7","Wallerstein's world-systems theory argues...",["Core countries benefit from unequal economic relationships with peripheral countries","All countries develop through identical stages","Climate alone determines national wealth","Countries develop independently of global trade"],"Core countries benefit from unequal economic relationships with peripheral countries","World-systems theory emphasizes structural unequal exchange: core economies tend to control higher-value activities, while peripheral economies can remain dependent on lower-value production and exports."],["Unit 7","Core countries in Wallerstein's model are usually...",["Wealthy, industrialized, and in control of high-value production","Poor and agricultural","Isolated from world trade","Without manufacturing"],"Wealthy, industrialized, and in control of high-value production","Core countries (like the US, Germany, Japan) dominate high-value manufacturing, finance, and technology."],["Unit 7","Outsourcing means...",["Contracting work to an outside company, often to reduce costs","Moving company operations to another country","Ending all international trade","Replacing service jobs with primary-sector work"],"Contracting work to an outside company, often to reduce costs","Outsourcing means hiring an external company to perform work. Offshoring means moving work to another country; a company can outsource domestically or outsource work to a firm abroad."]];
